---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const mkHref = (p) => {
  if (!p) return p;
  if (/^https?:\/\//.test(p)) return p;

  const cleaned = p.replace(/^\/+/, "");

  return `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${encodeURI(cleaned)}`;
};
---

<MainLayout title="Research — Federico D'Onofrio">

  <h1>Research</h1>

  <div class="research-intro">

    <p>
      My research combines intellectual history, the history of economics,
      and social history. It borders Science and Technology Studies and the
      history of science. The common thread is an interest in
      <strong> policy action and collective decision-making</strong>: how
      collective actors come to understand a situation, define what ought
      to be done, and act upon it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Collective decision-making is not simply the spontaneous aggregation
      of individual choices, but a process that requires forms of knowledge,
      authority, and judgement. While the literature has so far underlined
      the importance of models and other forms of representation as part of
      the "art of world-making", my principal concern is somewhat different:
      <strong> how information, expertise and values are brought together
      in processes of collective decision-making and policy action.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      So far, my research has investigated the production and use of
      information in 20th century European agriculture and in 18th century
      political economy, but it is now expanding to other areas and topics
      (European industrial policy, and the investigation of backwardness).
    </p>

  </div>


  <details class="research-section">
    <summary>Agriculture</summary>

    <div class="research-content">

      <p>
        A major part of my research has focused on the production of
        knowledge for agricultural policy, and on the different empirical
        practices, organisations and forms of expertise through which agriculture was made observable.
      </p>

      <p>
        In <em>Observing Agriculture in Early Twentieth-Century Italy</em>
        <a href="https://www.routledge.com/Observing-Agriculture-in-Early-Twentieth-Century-Italy-Agricultural-economists-and-statistics/DOnofrio/p/book/9781032402482">[--&gt;]</a>,
        I examined three principal modes through which agriculture was
        investigated and represented: <strong>enquiries, statistics and
        monographs</strong>. My subsequent research has extended this
        analysis in several directions.
      </p>

      <p>
        One concerns <strong>accountancy data</strong>, which became central
        to my work through the MSCA project FARMACCOUNTA
        <a href={mkHref("farmaccounta")}>[--&gt;]</a>
        and subsequently the ERC StG DATAREV
        <a href="https://datarev.univie.ac.at/">[--&gt;]</a>.
        Research into accounting led to the volume
        <em>Farm Accounts in Rural Europe, c. 1700–1914</em>
        <a href="https://boydellandbrewer.com/book/farm-accounts-in-rural-europe-c-1700-1914-hb/">[--&gt;]</a>,
        edited with Nathalie Joly
        <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Nathalie-Joly/3">[--&gt;]</a>,
        and to my paper on farm valuation
        <a href={mkHref("publications")}>[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Another concerns <strong>field observation and field trips</strong>,
        explored in my work on Wolf Ladejinsky
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1215/00182702-9779614">[--&gt;]</a>.
        A third concerns the development of
        <strong> international agricultural statistics</strong>, particularly
        through my work on the International Institute of Agriculture with
        Niccolò Mignemi
        <a href={mkHref("people#niccolo-mignemi")}>[--&gt;]</a>
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1353/jwh.2025.a974184">[--&gt;]</a>
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.4000/histoiremesure.19003">[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taken together, these strands describe the emergence of a
        <strong> "first data revolution in European agriculture"</strong>
        in the early 20th century: a transformation in the ways agricultural
        economies could be observed, represented, and organized, brought about
        by datafication. This revolution was the result of the conscious action
        of organizations and experts, as I showed in my work on accounting
        offices
        <a href={mkHref("publications")}>[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Finally, I have increasingly turned from the forms and institutions
        of agricultural knowledge to the <strong>experts themselves and the
        construction of expertise</strong>
        <a href={mkHref("papers/rise-of-agricultural-economics.pdf")}
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer">[--&gt;]</a>.
        A post in the blog of the Journal of the History of Ideas summarized
        the "ideology" of early 20th century agricultural economists
        <a href="https://www.jhiblog.org/2025/12/15/the-margins-of-the-field-rediscovering-agricultural-economists-for-the-history-of-ideas/">[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The conference
        <strong> Two Centuries of Agricultural Futures: Actors and Networks
        of Agricultural Policy-Making in Europe from the 1760s to the 1960s</strong>,
        held in July 2026, was co-organized with
        Myriam Lichani
        <a href={mkHref("people#myriam-lichani")}>[--&gt;]</a>
        and Filippo Soramel
        <a href={mkHref("people#filippo-soramel")}>[--&gt;]</a>.
        It was meant to mobilize other agricultural historians around this
        agenda.
        <a href={mkHref("conferences/datarev-workshop_two-centuries-of-agricultural-futures.pdf")}
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Conference programme and poster [--&gt;]
        </a>
      </p>

    </div>
  </details>


  <details class="research-section">
    <summary>The rise of political economy</summary>

    <div class="research-content">

      <p>
        At the origins of political economy, in the eighteenth century, lies
        a concrete concern with <strong>government action</strong>. The
        Neapolitan tradition of <em>Economia civile</em> articulated this
        concern in terms of <em>felicitas publica</em>
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S1053837215000401">[--&gt;]</a>
        and of the
        <strong>functions of government</strong>
        <a href="https://www.francoangeli.it/rivista/getArticoloFree/62420/En">[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Policy action, in this context, depended on the construction of an
        <strong> information space</strong> that was complex and articulated.
        My research on the administrative culture of Ancien Régime Naples has
        examined the conflicts surrounding the production, circulation and
        authority of information, particularly in relation to grain policy,
        in <em>Numeri falsi e numeri errati: le basi informative della
        politica granaria a Napoli nella seconda metà del secolo XVIII</em>
        <a href="https://cris.unibo.it/handle/11585/1031705">[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        My current research takes this question further by investigating the
        emergence, in the eighteenth century, of a distinctive attitude
        towards <strong>facts</strong>. Much of the existing literature has
        explored the relationship between political economy and the natural
        sciences, particularly Newtonian physics. My research instead explores
        the connections between economic knowledge and the
        <strong> antiquarian and philological traditions</strong>, asking how
        these traditions contributed to the emergence of what could count as
        an economic fact
        <a href={mkHref("papers/ESHET-2026-paper.pdf")}
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer">[--&gt;]</a>.
      </p>

    </div>
  </details>


  <details class="research-section">
    <summary>European industrial policy</summary>

    <div class="research-content">

      <p>
        Most work on the economic constitution of European institutions has
        focused on the creation of the <strong>Common Market and Single
        Market</strong>, and on the <strong>Economic and Monetary Union</strong>.
        My new research takes a different approach by examining more closely
        the developmental ambitions of European institutions and the ways in
        which they sought to shape investment and economic transformation.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am particularly interested in the <strong>investment priorities </strong>
        identified by European organisations: first through the activities of
        the <strong>European Investment Bank</strong>, and subsequently through
        initiatives such as the Trans-European Transport Network (TEN-T) and
        European regional policies.
      </p>

    </div>
  </details>


  <details class="research-section">
    <summary><em>Coup d'œil</em></summary>

    <div class="research-content">
<p>"But, to take the thing right, it should seem that our counsels and deliberations depend as much upon fortune as anything else we do, and that she engages also our arguments in her uncertainty and confusion. “We argue rashly and adventurously,” says Timaeus in Plato, “by reason that, as well as ourselves, our discourses have great participation in the temerity of chance." (Montaigne, <em>Of the uncertainty of our judgment</em>)</p>
      <p>
        Data are not all there is about decision. Actually, we can only rarely identify the bits of information which underpin specific decisions. I am therefore investigating the early-modern concept of
        <em> coup d'œil</em> as a way of understanding decision-making and
        practical judgement
        <a href={mkHref("coup-doeil")}>[--&gt;]</a>. The advance of Galilean and then Newtonian sciences, with their emphasis on geometry and mathematization, left the problem open of connecting human action to universal geometric rules. To military and art theorists of the 18th century, the <em>coup-d'oeil</em> offered a handy concept to describe the ability of generals and artists to apply and adapt rules to particular contexts.
      </p>

    </div>
  </details>


  <details class="research-section">
    <summary>Backwardness begins at home</summary>

    <div class="research-content">

      <p>
        
        My investigations on the role of experts and data led me to question how and why ambitious reform plans were launched and justified.  
        I thus plan a new project on the history of
        <strong> backwardness</strong>: how the modern concept emerged and
        became an object of policy intervention. Critical approaches to
        development studies have traditionally understood backwardness as the
        result of a foreign gaze upon an organic community, whether a village
        or a nation. My research instead explores how metrics of progress and
        backwardness may be constructed <strong>within the community
        itself</strong>, and how they can support strategies of catching up
        through reform, emulation and adaptation. Backwardness thus becomes
        not simply an external judgement, but an element in the political
        strategies of historical actors themselves.
      </p>

    </div>
  </details>


</MainLayout>